WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.216 --> 00:00:19.529
It was not unusual for Inspector Lestrade of Scotland Yard to spend the evening at our Baker Street

2
00:00:19.549 --> 00:00:25.234
lodgings. The great detective Sherlock Holmes welcomed these visits because they enabled him to keep up on everything

3
00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:26.815
that was happening at police headquarters.

4
00:00:27.856 --> 00:00:32.280
In return, Holmes was always ready to listen to the details of any case that was puzzling Lestrade.

5
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.444
My friend could sometimes give Lestrade a helpful suggestion without actually getting involved in the case.

6
00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:42.529
On this particular evening Lestrade had talked about the weather and the newspaper headlines.

7
00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:46.452
Then the inspector had fallen silent, puffing thoughtfully on his cigar.

8
00:00:47.233 --> 00:00:48.414
Holmes looked keenly at him.

9
00:00:48.434 --> 00:00:51.036
"Do you have any unusual cases right now?"

10
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:51.957
asked Holmes.

11
00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:53.539
"Oh, no, Mr.

12
00:00:53.579 --> 00:00:55.220
Holmes. Nothing at all."

13
00:00:55.921 --> 00:00:57.682
"Then tell me about it," urged Holmes.

14
00:00:58.523 --> 00:00:59.304
Lestrade laughed.

15
00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:01.324
"Well, Mr.

16
00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:04.245
Holmes, there's no use denying that something is on my mind.

17
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:08.346
And yet it's such an absurd business that I hesitate to bother you about it.

18
00:01:09.326 --> 00:01:11.867
In fact, the case may be of more interest to Dr.

19
00:01:11.887 --> 00:01:12.687
Watson than to you."

20
00:01:12.707 --> 00:01:14.608
"Does it involve an illness?"

21
00:01:15.048 --> 00:01:15.448
I asked.

22
00:01:15.468 --> 00:01:18.269
"Possibly insanity," said Lestrade.

23
00:01:18.289 --> 00:01:20.570
"And a strange form of insanity too.

24
00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.710
There is a man with such a strong hatred of Napoleon.

25
00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.471
He's destroying every statue of the emperor he sees."

26
00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:28.852
Holmes sank back into his chair.

27
00:01:29.648 --> 00:01:30.789
"That's not a case for me."

28
00:01:30.809 --> 00:01:34.610
"Exactly. That's what I thought," said Lestrade.

29
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:39.352
"But when the man commits burglary in order to break the statues, then the case becomes something for

30
00:01:39.372 --> 00:01:40.713
the police, not the doctor."

31
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:43.514
"Burglary!" Holmes sat up straight.

32
00:01:43.534 --> 00:01:45.614
"This is more interesting.

33
00:01:46.335 --> 00:01:47.275
Let me hear the details."

34
00:01:48.216 --> 00:01:49.776
Lestrade took out his official notebook.

35
00:01:51.157 --> 00:01:53.378
He refreshed his memory by glancing through his notes.

36
00:01:53.398 --> 00:01:58.400
"The first case was reported four days ago at the shop of Morse Hudson, a dealer in pictures

37
00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.040
and statues."

38
00:01:59.988 --> 00:02:04.412
Hudson's shop consisted of two rooms: the sales area at the front of the shop and a back

39
00:02:04.472 --> 00:02:06.213
room that served as an office and stockroom.

40
00:02:07.134 --> 00:02:10.517
The sales clerk had just stepped into the back room for a moment when he heard a crash

41
00:02:10.557 --> 00:02:11.477
from the front of the store.

42
00:02:12.418 --> 00:02:17.022
Hurrying out, he found a plaster bust of Napoleon lying in fragments on the counter, where it had

43
00:02:17.082 --> 00:02:18.543
stood with several other works of art.

44
00:02:19.444 --> 00:02:21.025
The clerk then rushed into the street.

45
00:02:21.786 --> 00:02:24.728
Several passersby said that they had noticed a man running from the shop.

46
00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:29.212
But the sales clerk could neither see the man nor find any way to identify him.

47
00:02:30.036 --> 00:02:33.957
It seemed to be one of those senseless acts of vandalism that occur from time to time, and

48
00:02:33.997 --> 00:02:36.058
that's how the sales clerk reported it to the police.

49
00:02:36.078 --> 00:02:41.039
"The plaster cast was not worth more than a few shillings," said Lestrade.

50
00:02:41.059 --> 00:02:44.360
"The whole affair didn't appear to be worth much investigation.

51
00:02:45.181 --> 00:02:48.341
The second case, however, was more serious and also more unusual.

52
00:02:49.222 --> 00:02:50.342
It occurred just last night."

53
00:02:51.182 --> 00:02:55.784
Lestrade said that within a few hundred yards of Morse Hudson's shop, there lived a well-known physician named

54
00:02:55.824 --> 00:02:57.524
Dr. Barnicot.

55
00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:02.105
His residence and main office were in the same building, but he also had a smaller, second office

56
00:03:02.145 --> 00:03:02.846
two miles away.

57
00:03:02.866 --> 00:03:04.306
"This Dr.

58
00:03:04.326 --> 00:03:09.928
Barnicot is an enthusiastic admirer of Napoleon, and his house is full of books, pictures, and historical objects

59
00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:12.029
relating to the emperor," explained Lestrade.

60
00:03:12.949 --> 00:03:17.390
A while ago the doctor purchased two identical plaster casts of a famous bust of Napoleon.

61
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:21.792
He placed one of these in the front hall of his house and the other on the mantel

62
00:03:21.852 --> 00:03:23.112
in his office two miles away.

63
00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:25.100
"When Dr.

64
00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:29.884
Barnicot came downstairs this morning, he was astonished to find that his house had been broken into overnight,"

65
00:03:30.244 --> 00:03:30.864
said Lestrade.

66
00:03:31.765 --> 00:03:34.387
"Nothing had been taken except the plaster cast in the front hall.

67
00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:38.329
It had been carried outside and dashed savagely against the garden wall.

68
00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:41.832
The broken pieces of the statue were found at the base of the wall."

69
00:03:42.652 --> 00:03:43.973
Holmes rubbed his hands together.

70
00:03:43.993 --> 00:03:46.635
"This is certainly very unusual."

71
00:03:46.655 --> 00:03:50.938
"I thought it would interest you, Holmes, but I'm not yet at the end of the story," said

72
00:03:50.978 --> 00:03:54.712
Lestrade. "After working at his main office in the morning, Dr.

73
00:03:54.732 --> 00:03:56.773
Barnicot was due at his second office at noon.

74
00:03:57.673 --> 00:04:01.594
Imagine his amazement when he arrived and found that a window had been opened during the night!

75
00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:07.116
Broken pieces of the second bust were strewn all over the room; it had been smashed to bits."

76
00:04:08.096 --> 00:04:10.937
"Was there anything to give you a clue about the person who had done the mischief?"

77
00:04:11.277 --> 00:04:11.857
Holmes asked.

78
00:04:11.877 --> 00:04:14.678
"No," said Lestrade.

79
00:04:14.698 --> 00:04:16.699
"Now that you have all the facts, Mr.

80
00:04:16.759 --> 00:04:18.099
Holmes, what do you make of them?"

81
00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:20.920
"They are unusual," admitted Holmes.

82
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.121
"Were the two busts belonging to Dr.

83
00:04:23.161 --> 00:04:26.183
Barnicot identical to the one that was destroyed in Morse Hudson's shop?"

84
00:04:26.203 --> 00:04:29.445
"They were made from the same mold," said Lestrade.

85
00:04:30.166 --> 00:04:31.406
"What do you think of the case, Dr.

86
00:04:31.426 --> 00:04:35.949
Watson?" "Anything can happen when someone becomes obsessed with one idea," I said.

87
00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:41.553
"A person may appear sane in every other way, and yet become fixated on one subject or one

88
00:04:41.593 --> 00:04:42.794
person such as Napoleon."

89
00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:46.156
"That doesn't explain this situation," said Holmes.

90
00:04:47.016 --> 00:04:51.479
"No matter how obsessed this person was with Napoleon, how could he have known where the busts were?"

91
00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:52.739
"Well, how do you explain it?"

92
00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:54.340
I asked him with some irritation.

93
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.202
"I don't attempt to explain it," answered Holmes.

94
00:04:59.123 --> 00:05:02.285
"I merely observe that there is a certain method in the man's eccentric actions.

95
00:05:03.145 --> 00:05:04.306
For example, in Dr.

96
00:05:04.346 --> 00:05:09.769
Barnicot's front hall, where a noise might awaken the family sleeping upstairs, the bust was taken outside before

97
00:05:09.809 --> 00:05:10.369
being broken.

98
00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:15.092
At the office, where there was less danger of anyone hearing anything, it was smashed right where it

99
00:05:15.152 --> 00:05:21.192
stood." Lestrade and I agreed that the whole affair seemed absurdly insignificant when compared to other crimes, but

100
00:05:21.252 --> 00:05:23.233
Holmes was not yet ready to dismiss the case.

101
00:05:23.253 --> 00:05:29.778
"When I consider that some of my most famous cases had the least promising beginnings, I don't dare

102
00:05:29.818 --> 00:05:31.759
to call anything trivial," said Holmes.

103
00:05:31.779 --> 00:05:37.103
"You will remember, Watson, how the dreadful business of the Abernetty family was brought to my attention by

104
00:05:37.163 --> 00:05:39.865
how deep the parsley had sunk into the butter on a hot day.

105
00:05:40.845 --> 00:05:44.168
I can't afford, therefore, to smile at your three broken busts, Lestrade.

106
00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.061
I shall be very much obliged if you will let me hear of any fresh developments."

107
00:05:50.021 --> 00:05:54.022
The next development came more quickly, and in a more tragic form, than Holmes could have imagined.

108
00:05:54.883 --> 00:05:58.123
I was still dressing in my bedroom the next morning when there was a tap on my door.

109
00:05:59.044 --> 00:06:00.744
Holmes entered with a telegram in his hand.

110
00:06:01.524 --> 00:06:07.426
He read it aloud: Come instantly, 131 Pitt Street, Kensington.–Lestrade "What does it mean?"

111
00:06:07.846 --> 00:06:09.146
I asked, hurrying into my jacket.

112
00:06:09.166 --> 00:06:10.567
"I don't know.

113
00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:14.808
It could be anything, but I suspect there are new developments in the story of the busts.

114
00:06:15.784 --> 00:06:18.827
In that case our vandal has begun operations in another part of London."

115
00:06:19.688 --> 00:06:20.769
Holmes turned to leave my room.

116
00:06:20.789 --> 00:06:24.313
"There's coffee on the table, Watson, and I have a cab waiting at the door."
